<h2>Warehouse space planning calculator</h2><p>This estimator starts with inventory volume rather than an arbitrary square-foot-per-unit rule. It converts units to pallet equivalents, applies a peak/growth buffer, divides by effective vertical storage levels, and then expands the storage footprint to a gross warehouse area.</p>
<h2>Calculation method</h2><p><strong>Buffered pallets = ceil((inventory ÷ units per pallet) × (1 + buffer))</strong>. Floor pallet positions are buffered pallets divided by effective levels and rounded up. Storage footprint equals floor positions times pallet footprint. Gross area equals that footprint divided by the share of the building that can actually be dedicated to storage footprints.</p>
<h2>Why gross area is larger than pallet footprint</h2><p>Warehouses need aisles, staging, receiving, shipping, packing, battery/charging areas, fire egress, columns, offices and other non-storage space. The storage-share input is therefore an explicit planning assumption, not a universal benchmark.</p>
<h2>Validate cube and handling constraints</h2><p>Effective levels depend on clear height, rack type, product height, sprinkler clearances, equipment, load ratings and SKU rules. Fast-moving operations may intentionally operate below maximum storage density to protect throughput. Use this output for early sizing, then complete a layout and code/safety review.</p>
